---
import InsertLayout from '../layouts/InsertLayout.astro';
import { breadcrumbSchema } from '../utils/jsonld';

const jsonLd = breadcrumbSchema([
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'Fixed-scope engagements', url: '/engagements' }
]);
---

<InsertLayout
  title="Fixed-scope engagements"
  description="Three bounded pieces of work with published prices, defined acceptance tests, and payment after you verify the result."
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
  heading="Fixed-scope engagements"
  subheading="Published prices. Defined acceptance. You pay after it passes."
>
  <p>
    Most technical work is quoted after a discovery call, a scoping document and
    a proposal. That is three weeks before anyone writes a line of code, and it
    is how a small, well-defined problem ends up costing more to specify than to
    solve.
  </p>
  <p>
    These three engagements are bounded on purpose. The scope is written down,
    the price is published, and the acceptance test is agreed before work starts
    — so you can decide in one reply instead of three meetings.
  </p>

  <h2>1. File conversion — US$850</h2>
  <p>
    You have an export from a system you are leaving, or a file in a shape
    nothing else will read. You need it in a schema something else accepts,
    and you need to trust it.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>the converted file, in the target schema you specify</li>
    <li>
      complete source-row accounting: every input row either appears in the
      output or appears in a reject list with the reason it was rejected. The
      counts reconcile, or the run fails rather than quietly publishing
    </li>
    <li>a written policy for duplicates and for rows that are ambiguous</li>
    <li>one command that regenerates the whole output from the source</li>
    <li>tests you specify and can run yourself</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>Bounded at:</strong> one source format, one target format, up to
    50,000 rows. Larger or multi-format work is quoted separately.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Integration triage — US$1,200, refunded if we cannot reproduce it</h2>
  <p>
    Something that used to work is failing intermittently, silently, or only in
    production. Before anyone should be paid to fix it, someone has to prove
    what is actually wrong.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>a reproduction of the failure, or a written statement that it could not be reproduced and what that rules out</li>
    <li>root cause with the evidence that establishes it — logs, request bytes, timing, whatever actually demonstrates it</li>
    <li>a fix plan with acceptance criteria you can check</li>
    <li>a written risk note on what else the same cause is likely affecting</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <strong>If we cannot reproduce the failure, this is refunded in full.</strong>
    We would rather return the fee than hand you a confident guess.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Reproducible test or evaluation harness — US$2,200</h2>
  <p>
    You have a system whose output quality you assert but cannot re-verify on
    demand — a model, a classifier, a matching or extraction step. You need a
    number you can defend, and regenerate next quarter without paying for it
    twice.
  </p>
  <p><strong>What you get:</strong></p>
  <ul>
    <li>a scoring harness that runs against saved outputs, so a published result can be re-derived offline without re-running the system that produced it</li>
    <li>results that are byte-stable: identical inputs give identical output, every time</li>
    <li>explicit accounting for the awkward cases — abstentions, unparseable output, missing and duplicate records — reported separately rather than collapsed into one number</li>
    <li>a self-contained viewer, and documentation for your team to add cases without us</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How payment works</h2>
  <p>
    We agree the scope and the acceptance test in writing first. You pay after
    the acceptance test passes, not before. For a first engagement, that means
    your risk is your own review time.
  </p>
  <p>
    Prices are in US dollars and exclude any tax applicable in your
    jurisdiction. Larger work is quoted as milestones against the same
    principle: each one is defined, checkable and paid on acceptance.
  </p>

  <h2>What we actually work with</h2>
  <p>
    So you can rule us in or out without a conversation:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Languages and runtimes:</strong> Python, JavaScript and TypeScript,
      Node, SQL, shell. Astro for static sites.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Data work:</strong> CSV, TSV, fixed-width, JSON and XML in or out;
      schema mapping and conversion; reconciliation against a source of truth;
      deduplication with a stated policy; Excel output.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Integration work:</strong> REST and webhook integrations, request
      signing, retry and idempotency behaviour, error handling and failure
      queues, API debugging against real traffic.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Testing:</strong> automated test suites, reproducible evaluation
      harnesses, adversarial and failure-path testing.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Not in this list means we would be learning it on your budget, and we would
    tell you so rather than take the work.
  </p>

  <h2>What would make these a bad fit</h2>
  <p>
    Worth saying plainly, because a mismatch discovered later is expensive for
    both of us:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>If you need someone on calls</strong> — standups, live debugging
      sessions, stakeholder meetings — this is not that. The work is
      asynchronous and delivered in writing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If you need regulatory certification</strong> — accessibility
      conformance statements, security attestations, clinical or financial
      sign-off — we do not issue those.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If the problem is not yet defined</strong>, a fixed price is the
      wrong instrument. Say so and we will scope it honestly rather than sell
      you a number.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>If you need a specific platform we have no track record in</strong>,
      we will tell you rather than learn on your budget.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Evidence, not adjectives</h2>
  <p>
    Rather than describe how carefully we work, the
    <a href="/proofs">working samples</a> are published with full source, their
    test records, and their stated limits. They are built on invented data and
    are demonstrations of method rather than client references — the pages say
    so themselves.
  </p>
  <p>
    Toledo Technologies LLC is the contracting supplier. How we operate is set
    out on the <a href="/about">about page</a>.
  </p>
  <h2>Starting one</h2>
  <p>
    Email <a href="mailto:dev@toledotechnologies.com">dev@toledotechnologies.com</a>
    with the shape of the task. Useful things to include, if you have them: the
    source format, what the output needs to be, and how you would know it worked.
    Redacted samples are fine and often faster than a description.
  </p>
  <p>
    You will get a written answer with a scope, a price and the acceptance test
    we would be held to. If it does not fit, the reply will say so and why —
    that is a normal outcome and it costs you nothing.
  </p>
</InsertLayout>
